# CODEOWNERS — security-sensitive paths.
#
# Security model: docs/security/ci-security-model.md (D7, D11)
#
# IMPORTANT — what this file does and does not do:
#
#   * It does NOT prevent a pull request from executing. Workflows from
#     first-time external fork contributors require manual approval before they
#     run (D2, Option B); same-repository pull requests are not gated. Approval
#     authorizes workflow execution only, not acceptance or merge.
#   * It does NOT enforce anything by itself. GitHub only requests Code Owner
#     review when branch protection or a ruleset on the target branch is
#     configured to require it.
#   * It does NOT replace human review. It routes review to the right person.
#
# Effective enforcement therefore requires the repository settings in
# docs/security/ci-security-model.md §7. Those settings are NOT changeable from
# a repository file and were NOT changed by M5.0 or M5.1.
#
# Single-maintainer governance (GOV-1). This file is retained as *ownership and
# responsibility metadata*: it names who is accountable for each sensitive path.
# It does NOT gate merges on its own, and mandatory Code Owner approval is
# DISABLED while the project has only one maintainer. With a single owner, a
# mandatory Code Owner approval is unsatisfiable: the platform forbids authors
# approving their own pull requests.
#
# What replaces it: security-sensitive changes must receive an explicit Owner
# self-review before merge, recorded in the pull request description (what was
# reviewed; what verification was performed and observed; what was not verified;
# and an acknowledgement that the review was self-review, not independent
# review). Self-review is NOT independent review and must never be described as
# such. The same Owner can modify both a workflow and the tests that validate it,
# so self-review plus CI do not eliminate false-green CI (residual risk R3).
#
# Mandatory Code Owner approval is deferred, not abolished. It is to be
# re-enabled when a qualified independent reviewer becomes available; that
# reviewer must then be added to the rules below.

# CODEOWNERS must cover itself. If it did not, the protection it provides
# could be removed by the very pull request it is meant to gate.
